# B2 First: ek okuma çalışmaları (özgün metinler)
def mk(k,id,t,ty,nm,ins,**o):
    d={'k':k,'id':id,'t':t,'ty':ty,'nm':nm,'ti':'Reading and Use of English Part '+k[1],'ins':ins};d.update(o);return d
CI='Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.'
R=[
mk('R5','b2r5a','Uzun metin: Yetişkin olarak dil öğrenmek','mc','Okuma 5 · Uzun metin','Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.',title='Learning a language later in life',
 p=['When I turned forty, I decided to learn Portuguese. My partner\'s family comes from Lisbon, and after years of nodding politely at dinner parties, I felt it was time to understand what was actually being said. I assumed that, as an adult with a good education, I would make quick progress. I could not have been more wrong.',
  'The first few months were humbling. Children, it seems, absorb a language almost without effort, whereas I had to struggle with every verb ending. My teacher, a patient woman called Inês, explained that adults learn differently: we are better at understanding rules, but worse at copying sounds. "Stop translating in your head," she kept telling me. "Just listen."',
  'That advice changed everything. I started listening to radio programmes while I cooked, and I stuck notes on everything in the flat. At first, the words slid past me like a river, but gradually I began to catch individual phrases. The turning point came when I understood a joke told by my partner\'s grandfather, and the whole table laughed with me instead of at me.',
  'I am still far from fluent, and I doubt that I ever will be. But I no longer see mistakes as failures. They are, in fact, the proof that I am trying. If there is one lesson I would pass on to other late learners, it is this: do not wait until you feel ready. You will never feel ready, and the language is waiting for you anyway.'],
 q=[{'q':'What does the writer say about his expectations at the beginning?','o':['He thought learning would be easy for an educated adult.','He was afraid that his partner\'s family would laugh at him.','He expected to need many years before speaking.','He had learnt other languages quickly before.'],'a':0,'tg':['attitude'],'e':'"I assumed ... I would make quick progress. I could not have been more wrong."'},
  {'q':'According to Inês, why do adults find language learning difficult?','o':['They are not interested in grammar.','They are less able to imitate sounds.','They have too little time to practise.','They are afraid of making mistakes.'],'a':1,'tg':['detail'],'e':'Yetişkinler kuralları daha iyi anlar ama sesleri taklitte daha zayıftır.'},
  {'q':'What does "the words slid past me like a river" suggest?','o':['He found the vocabulary beautiful.','He could not separate the words he heard.','He was listening to the radio too loudly.','He felt calm while listening.'],'a':1,'tg':['vocab','inference'],'e':'Kelimeler akıp gidiyordu; tek tek ayırt edemiyordu.'},
  {'q':'What was the turning point for the writer?','o':['Passing a language exam','Understanding a joke at the family table','Moving to Lisbon','Reading a book in Portuguese'],'a':1,'tg':['detail'],'e':'Büyükbabanın şakasını anlayıp masayla birlikte güldü.'},
  {'q':'What is the writer\'s attitude to mistakes now?','o':['He tries to avoid them at all costs.','He thinks they show how little he knows.','He accepts them as a sign of effort.','He believes teachers should correct them immediately.'],'a':2,'tg':['attitude'],'e':'"They are the proof that I am trying."'}]),
mk('R5','b2r5b','Uzun metin: Mahallenin ana caddesi','mc','Okuma 5 · Uzun metin','Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.',title='The future of the high street',
 p=['Walk through almost any town centre today and you will see the same pattern: empty shops with "to let" signs in the windows, next to a handful of coffee chains and phone stores. Many commentators blame online shopping, and there is no doubt that sales on the internet have increased dramatically. However, the real picture is more complicated than the headlines suggest.',
  'In some places, the high street is flourishing. In a market town in the north of England, the council gave cheap rents to small independent businesses on the condition that they offered something local. Within three years, a bakery, a bookshop and a repair café had opened, and the number of visitors on Saturdays had doubled. "People don\'t come to the high street just to buy things any more," says the town\'s manager, "they come for the experience."',
  'Yet not every initiative has succeeded. Another town spent a large sum on new paving and flower baskets, hoping that a prettier centre would attract shoppers. It did not. The car park was still expensive, the buses were infrequent, and the rents remained too high for small traders. As one shopkeeper put it, "You can\'t eat flowers."',
  'The lesson seems to be that high streets survive when they offer what the internet cannot: personal service, a sense of community and places to meet. Rather than competing with online giants, successful towns are learning to complement them.'],
 q=[{'q':'What is the writer\'s main point in the first paragraph?','o':['Online shopping is the only cause of the problem.','The situation is not as simple as many people say.','Coffee chains are better than independent shops.','Town centres will soon disappear completely.'],'a':1,'tg':['gist'],'e':'"the real picture is more complicated than the headlines suggest"'},
  {'q':'What condition did the northern council set for cheap rents?','o':['The shops had to open on Sundays.','The businesses had to be run by local people.','The businesses had to offer something local.','The owners had to repair old buildings.'],'a':2,'tg':['detail'],'e':'"on the condition that they offered something local"'},
  {'q':'Why did the second town\'s plan fail?','o':['The flowers died quickly.','Basic problems such as parking and rents remained.','Too many shops closed in the first year.','Shoppers preferred the old paving.'],'a':1,'tg':['detail','inference'],'e':'Otopark pahalı, otobüsler seyrek, kiralar yüksek kaldı.'},
  {'q':'What does the shopkeeper mean by "You can\'t eat flowers"?','o':['Decoration does not solve real economic needs.','Flower shops are too expensive.','Food is more important than shopping.','Customers do not like flowers.'],'a':0,'tg':['vocab','inference'],'e':'Süs, esnafın gerçek sorunlarını çözmez.'},
  {'q':'What does the writer conclude?','o':['High streets should copy online shops.','Successful towns offer things the internet cannot.','Councils should stop spending money.','Personal service is no longer important.'],'a':1,'tg':['purpose'],'e':'Son paragraf: internetin veremediğini sunan caddeler hayatta kalıyor.'}]),
mk('R6','b2r6a','Cümle yerleştirme: Okul yemekhanesi','gap','Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme','Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.',title='A revolution in the school canteen',
 p='When Chef Marta Silva took over the kitchen at a large secondary school, she faced a difficult challenge. Most students were choosing chips and sugary drinks, and a lot of the healthy food was ending up in the bin. (1) ______\nHer first step was to talk to the students. She asked them what they liked and what they found boring. (2) ______ She used their ideas to redesign the menu, with names such as "Super Power Bowl" instead of "vegetable salad".\nThe second change was practical. Queues had always been long, so many students gave up and bought snacks from the shop. (3) ______ Now they can order on their phones and collect their meals in seconds.\nThe results surprised everyone. Within a year, the amount of waste had fallen by half. (4) ______ Several teachers even reported that afternoon lessons had become calmer.\nNot everything has been easy. (5) ______ Marta, however, remains hopeful: "Change takes time, but taste is something you can teach." Other schools are now asking her for advice. (6) ______',
 opts=[['A','Some parents complained that the new meals were more expensive.'],['B','She has already agreed to visit three of them next month.'],['C','To solve this, she introduced a simple ordering system.'],['D','Many of them said that the food looked unattractive.'],['E','This was not just a problem of taste, but of habits.'],['F','Students also seemed more energetic in the afternoons.'],['G','The canteen had been built in the 1960s.']],
 a=['E','D','C','F','A','B'],tg=[['coherence'],['reference','coherence'],['coherence'],['coherence'],['reference'],['coherence']],
 e=['"This was not just a problem of taste" önceki sorunları özetler (E).','"Many of them" = students; yiyecekler çekici görünmüyor (D).','Uzun kuyruklar sorun; "To solve this" ile çözüm (C).','Atık azaldı; öğrenciler öğleden sonra daha enerjik (F).','"Not everything has been easy": pahalı olduğuna dair şikâyet (A).','Diğer okullar tavsiye istiyor; "them" = okullar (B).']),
mk('R1','b2r1c','Çoktan seçmeli boşluk: Sessiz diskolar','cloze','Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk',CI,title='Silent discos',
 p='Silent discos, where people dance to music through wireless headphones, have become (1) ______ popular at festivals and student parties. The idea is simple: instead of loudspeakers, every guest (2) ______ a pair of headphones with two or three channels, and each channel plays a different style of music. The dance floor is quiet to anyone who isn\'t wearing a pair, which makes it (3) ______ funny to watch.\nOne major advantage is that the event does not (4) ______ the neighbours. In cities with strict noise rules, organisers can therefore hold parties in parks and squares late at night. Another benefit is that people can (5) ______ the volume to suit themselves, so those who prefer a quieter evening can still join in.\nCritics, (6) ______, say that wearing headphones separates people from one another. They argue that dancing is a shared experience, and that you lose the atmosphere if everybody listens to something different. Nevertheless, (7) ______ the number of silent discos has grown steadily, it seems that most people (8) ______ the idea.',
 o=[(['increasingly','extremely','equally','highly'],0,['collocation']),(['is given','gives','has given','is giving'],0,['passive-voice']),(['particularly','entirely','absolutely','simply'],0,['collocation']),(['disturb','interrupt','prevent','damage'],0,['collocation']),(['adjust','adapt','arrange','repair'],0,['collocation']),(['however','therefore','meanwhile','otherwise'],0,['linkers-contrast-purpose']),(['since','unless','whereas','although'],0,['linkers-contrast-purpose']),(['welcome','accept','enjoy','approve'],2,['collocation'])],
 e=['become increasingly popular: gitgide yaygınlaşmak.','every guest is given headphones: pasif.','particularly funny: özellikle komik.','disturb the neighbours: komşuları rahatsız etmek.','adjust the volume: sesi ayarlamak.','Critics, however, say...: ama eleştirmenler ...','since the number has grown: ... -den beri artıyor.','welcome the idea: fikri memnuniyetle karşılamak (enjoy the idea doğal bir kalıp değil).']),
mk('R3','b2r3a','Açık boşluk: Kısa mola','open','Okuma 3 · Açık boşluk','Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.',title='The power of taking breaks',one=True,
 p='Many people believe that working without a break is the best way to get things done. In fact, research suggests the opposite. When we concentrate (1) ______ a long time, our attention starts to fall, and we make more mistakes. A short break every hour can help us (2) ______ fresh and productive.\nOne study followed office workers for a month. Those (3) ______ took regular breaks were not only happier but also finished their tasks faster. It seems that even a five-minute walk can make a big difference, as long (4) ______ you leave your phone behind.\nUnfortunately, many employees feel guilty when they stop working. They are afraid that colleagues will think they (5) ______ lazy. Yet companies (6) ______ encourage breaks often discover that their staff become more creative. If you have never tried it, (7) ______ not start tomorrow? You may be surprised (8) ______ how much better you feel.',
 a=[['for'],['stay','remain','keep','feel','be'],['who','that'],['as'],['are','were'],['that','which','who'],['why'],['at','by']],
 tg=[['prepositions-in-on-at'],['gerund-infinitive'],['relative-clauses'],['first-conditional'],['present-simple'],['relative-clauses'],['question-words'],['prepositions-in-on-at']],
 e=['concentrate for a long time: uzun süre.','help us stay fresh: bize ... kalmada yardım eder.','Those who took breaks: ... alanlar.','as long as: -dığı sürece.','think they are lazy: tembel olduklarını düşünürler.','companies that encourage breaks: ... teşvik eden şirketler.','why not start tomorrow?: neden yarın başlamıyorsun?','surprised at/by: şaşırmak.']),
]
